## Instructions

1. Decide which repositories this story touches. Read `/story` for the contract, then read the code
   of every repository you plan to touch — clone the ones that are not already here. Plans grounded
   in real code beat plans written from descriptions. A story touching a single repository is fine: the
   plan is then a single issue.
2. For each repository, draft one issue: copy its source exactly from the catalog, then give its
   target owner, target repo, title, and body.

Issue-body rules (each agent sees ONLY its own repository):
- Write bodies high-level: goal, expected behaviour and contract, acceptance criteria. The agent
  plans the exact files itself.
- Acceptance criteria must state the expected tests: which behaviours need coverage and at what level
  (unit, integration, end-to-end), following the conventions already present in that repository.
- Spell out cross-component touchpoints explicitly: API shapes, endpoint paths, queue and message
  names, config keys. Sibling agents cannot see each other's repositories.
- NO sequencing notes in bodies ("after X merges") — ordering is expressed only through dependsOn.
- Do NOT reference the story or the other issues. A link back to the story is appended to every issue
  body automatically, and an agent reading its issue cannot open anything you point it at anyway.
3. Express ordering as dependsOn: a list of ZERO-BASED indexes into your issues array. An issue is
   only handed to an agent once every issue it depends on is finished.
4. Prefer a reasonable assumption stated in the summary over blocking on an open question.
5. Keep everything proportional to the story: a small story with clear instructions gets a
   one-or-two-sentence summary and short issue bodies. Detail is for stories that genuinely span
   components or carry real design decisions — padding a simple story makes the plan harder to
   review, not safer.

Return the plan as structured output: a summary of 2–4 sentences on the approach and how the pieces
fit together, and the issues themselves (source, owner, repo, title, body, dependsOn).
